Use Phalcon’s `SoftDelete` behavior. Add it to the model and configure:

- `field`: the flag column to update
- `value`: the value representing a deleted record

```php
<?php

use Phalcon\Mvc\Model;
use Phalcon\Mvc\Model\Behavior\SoftDelete;

class Invoices extends Model
{
    public $inv_deleted_flag;

    public function initialize()
    {
        $this->addBehavior(
            new SoftDelete(
                [
                    'field' => 'inv_deleted_flag',
                    'value' => 1,
                ]
            )
        );
    }
}
```

Then delete the model normally:

```php
Invoices::findFirst(2)->delete();
```

Instead of removing the row, Phalcon updates `inv_deleted_flag` to `1` [1]. The behavior marks records as deleted by changing a flag column rather than permanently deleting them [2].

You must explicitly include the deleted-status condition in queries; `SoftDelete` does not filter deleted records automatically [1]. Also, because the record is not physically deleted, the model’s `afterDelete` event is not triggered [1].

