Use `Phalcon\Mvc\Model\Transaction\Manager` to create a transaction, bind each model to it with `setTransaction()`, and commit only after all operations succeed. Roll back when any `save()` fails. [1][3]

```php
use MyApp\Models\Customers;
use MyApp\Models\Invoices;
use Phalcon\Mvc\Model\Transaction\Manager;
use Phalcon\Mvc\Model\Transaction\Failed;

try {
    $transactionManager = new Manager();
    $transaction = $transactionManager->get();

    $customer = new Customers();
    $customer->setTransaction($transaction);
    $customer->cst_name_last  = 'Vader';
    $customer->cst_name_first = 'Darth';

    if ($customer->save() === false) {
        $transaction->rollback('Cannot save Customer');
    }

    $invoice = new Invoices();
    $invoice->setTransaction($transaction);
    $invoice->inv_cst_id      = $customer->cst_id;
    $invoice->inv_status_flag = 1;
    $invoice->inv_title       = 'Invoice for ACME Inc.';
    $invoice->inv_total       = 100;

    if ($invoice->save() === false) {
        $transaction->rollback('Cannot save Invoice');
    }

    $transaction->commit();
} catch (Failed $e) {
    echo 'Failed, reason: ', $e->getMessage();
}
```

All participating models use the transaction’s unique connection, so the operations are committed as one unit or rolled back together. A transaction acts on a single database connection; it does not protect operations across different database connections. [2][3]

