Define the route with a name using `setName()`, then pass that name through the `for` key to `Phalcon\Mvc\Url::get()`. Route parameters are supplied in the same array, so the path does not need to be hard-coded [2][4].

```php
use Phalcon\Mvc\Router;
use Phalcon\Mvc\Url;

$router = new Router();

$router
    ->add('/portal/invoices/edit/{id}', [
        'controller' => 'invoices',
        'action'     => 'edit',
    ])
    ->setName('invoices-edit');

$url = new Url();

echo $url->get([
    'for' => 'invoices-edit',
    'id'  => 1,
]);
// /portal/invoices/edit/1
```

Additional key/value pairs passed as the second argument become query-string parameters [2]:

```php
echo $url->get(
    ['for' => 'invoices-edit', 'id' => 1],
    ['tab' => 'details']
);
// /portal/invoices/edit/1?tab=details
```

If the named route has a hostname, `get()` generates a protocol-relative URL such as `//account.company.com/dashboard` [1].

